#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055936 :tutorial/content "The **greatest common factor** (or $\\textrm{GCF}$) of $12$ and $18x$ is the largest expression that will divide *both* $12$ and $18x$ with no remainder.\n\nTo find the GCF, we first decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n18x &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3_(―) \\\\\n18x &= 2_(―) \\cdot 3_(―) \\cdot 3 \\cdot x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take one factor from each pair and multiply.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(12,18x) = {\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}3} = 6\n$$\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $12$ and $18x$ is $6$." :tutorial/id #uuid "b2b71877-6097-5d59-a2b8-7440bd0d5329" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1360 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
